Original commit body from 7d8641a0f594e3634a69639563a82fe27d45ffba:

Follow-up to the INV-pusin fix in this branch, plus one INHERITED red test.

check-schema-coverage failed on the parent commit with two NEW uncovered
values -- Edge.type=module_attr_ref and
Edge.evidence_type=module_attribute_reference. The cause is the fix working:
the schema-coverage corpus is one Rust file whose ONLY module_attr_ref edges
came from `use std::collections::HashMap;`, which the analyzer emitted as an
attribute read by mistake. The coverage was real and the thing it covered was
a producer artifact.

FIXED IN THE CORPUS, NOT IN THE BASELINE. Running with --update-baseline would
have accepted a permanent loss of coverage for a live edge type in exchange for
nothing. The fixture now carries a GENUINE module-attribute read
(`std::env::consts::OS`) -- the shape those two enum values are supposed to
describe, and one the I/O catalogue can actually classify
(`module: std::env, attributes: [consts]` -> env_read). Coverage returns to
parity: no new uncovered values, no new schema violations.

SEPARATELY, AN INHERITED FAILURE THIS BRANCH DID NOT CAUSE, fixed because
observing it makes it mine: tests/test_top_level_test_map.py's
`test_no_new_unreachable_top_level_tests` has been red on `dev` since
cb6498171e added tests/test_ci_self_claims_gate_scope.py without wiring it into
the map. A/B'd against a clean origin/dev checkout to confirm it is not this
branch's doing. Its subjects are the two WOODPECKER PIPELINE FILES, which are
not top-level sources and cannot be name-mapped, so it is allowlisted -- with
the per-PR gap stated rather than implied, exactly as the neighbouring entries
do.

